From be2671e9e556d25b170334f4cc6cac5aa6d8dfb4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Trung=20L=C3=AA?= <8@tle.id.au>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 16:19:24 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 29/29] Enable Widevine EME on Linux ppc64

The x86_64 Widevine CDM is run through a native proxy CDM that forwards the
Chromium CDM interface to a helper process under box64. Listing widevine in
eme_choices makes the ppc64 build define MOZ_WIDEVINE_EME, register the
com.widevine.alpha key system and carry the gmp-widevinecdm preferences.

The GMP child starts its sandbox before it loads the plugin library and the
seccomp filter would be inherited by anything the plugin forked, so on Linux
the child now starts a gmp-helper program found in the plugin directory just
before the sandbox engages, with one end of a socketpair as its argument, and
publishes the other end to the plugin in MOZ_GMP_HELPER_FD. The helper is as
trusted as the plugin library and runs in the child's network and IPC
namespaces. Plugins without a gmp-helper are unaffected.

The update servers do not serve Widevine for ppc64, so the local source
config aliases Linux_ppc64-gcc3 to the x86_64 package and the install manager
always consults that config for the plugin on little-endian ppc64. After the
package is extracted the downloaded library is moved aside as
libwidevinecdm-x86_64.so and the proxy library, its helper, the gmp-helper
launcher and the x64lib runtime are copied in from the directory named by
media.gmp-widevinecdm.proxy-dir, by default widevine-proxy under the
application directory. The Chromium update service is asked for the x64
package on that platform as well.

A CDM that fails to instantiate is fatal for the GMP child, so a proxy CDM
whose helper cannot run would turn a missing box64 into a plugin crash on
first use. GMPUtils.proxyCdmProblem checks that gmp-helper, wvhost and the
box64 the launcher will run are executable, box64 being taken from PATH
unless WV_BOX64 names one, as it is packaged separately from the proxy. The
install manager fails the install with that reason and GMPProvider leaves an
installed plugin unregistered, logging the reason, so key system requests get
NotSupportedError instead.

Two update checks can run together at startup (a version change and a due
check), and the second would re-extract the package into a directory that a
plugin started by the first is already using, so installs of the same addon
version now share one in-flight install.
